# Names only, no values. One secret per root-only file (0600) in /etc/bulig, loaded by systemd LoadCredential= and read by the program
# from $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY. Set each with:  deploy/set-secret.sh --credential <name>  (value on stdin, never on a command line)
#
# The kernel (bulig.service, user bulig) gets:
#   config          /etc/bulig/config.json: the kernel's config (root, 0600). Put there by release.sh the first time, from bulig.config.vps.json
#   pg-url          postgres://bulig:<generated>@127.0.0.1:5436/bulig   written by setup-cluster.sh (explicit host and port)
#   gh-token        GitHub token of the bot account (Keychain item bulig-github-bot on the Mac)
#   worker-tokens   the table of per-worker tokens, one "id=token" line each:  set-secret.sh --worker-token <id>
#
# The worker (bulig-worker.service, user bulig-worker) gets:
#   claude-token    from `claude setup-token` run as bulig-worker (interactive, Jasher only)
#   worker-token    this worker's own token for the kernel gateway; set together with its line in worker-tokens by
#                   `set-secret.sh --worker-token vps`
#
# The restore drill and the backup (root, short-lived oneshot units) get:
#   drill-pw        password of the bulig_drill role (not a superuser); written by setup-cluster.sh
#   pg-url          as above
#   backup-env      /etc/bulig/backup.env, the R2 settings (see backup.env.example)
#
# The claude-token is NOT in the kernel's unit, and the database URL, GitHub token and config are NOT in the worker's. They are separate
# Linux users with separate units, and /etc/bulig is root-only, so Claude Code (which runs as bulig-worker) cannot read the kernel's secrets.
#
# Added at cutover only, never before. Two kernels must never poll one Telegram bot:
#   telegram-token  Keychain item bulig-telegram-bot on the Mac (add it to the known names in lib/common.sh and to bulig.service)
